  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bilateral M&amp;D and NSD Support to RRC</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Following the escalation of the conflict in Ukraine on 24 February 2022, it is estimated that almost 6 million displaced people, including third-country nationals, have crossed the border into Romania from Ukraine and Moldova. As of May 2024, Romania hosts over 77,900 refugees from Ukraine, with around 161,300 registered for Temporary Protection or similar national schemes. As the emergency evolves into a more protracted stage, those who remain in Romania are facing challenges extending beyond immediate basic needs to encompass socio-economic integration and comprehensive protection.&#xa0;

Through BRC&apos;s scoping visit in July and active participation in Romanian Red Cross (RRC)&apos;s Unified Planning process, a comprehensive approach is recognized as crucial to enable sustained self-sufficiency/support of refugees in new host communities. This is a multiyear bilateral partnership with RRC to support people displaced by the Ukraine Crisis in Romania and other vulnerable groups of people on the move as well as the local population in Romania, who have also been indirectly impacted by the Ukraine crisis. This grant also aims to support RRC&#x2019;s National Society Development objectives, to build RRC capacity and increase the relevance and quality of humanitarian assistance provided by RRC to affected populations.</narrative>
  </description>
